---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CASES } from '../data/cases.ts';
import { TRACKS, SOLVED_ON, ENSEMBLE_MEMBERS } from '../data/generated/tracks.ts';
import { GYRES, JETS, DRIFTERS } from '../lib/ocean.ts';
import { fmtDate, STATUSES, CLASSES } from '../lib/registry.ts';
import { LAND_MASK_W, LAND_MASK_H } from '../data/generated/landmask.ts';

const residuals = Object.values(TRACKS)
  .map((t) => t.skill)
  .filter((n): n is number => n !== null)
  .sort((a, b) => a - b);
const medianResidual = residuals[Math.floor(residuals.length / 2)];
const dissenting = CASES.filter((c) => TRACKS[c.code].dissent);

const basisCounts = (['documented', 'reconstructed', 'speculative'] as const).map((b) => ({
  b,
  n: CASES.filter((c) => c.basis === b).length,
}));
---

<Base
  title="The Office"
  description="What the Drift Registry is, how the model works, what is real and what is invented, and who built it."
  nav="about"
>
  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-bottom:var(--sp-6)">
    <div class="head">
      <div class="head__meta">
        <p class="label">The Office</p>
        <p class="label">Ilwaco, Washington · est. 1974</p>
      </div>
      <h1 class="a__h">An archive for things that are still moving</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <p class="lede">
        Most archives hold objects. This one holds positions, and the objects are elsewhere.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Office of Pelagic Recovery keeps case files on things that went into the sea and did
          not stop. It records where each one entered the water, what it was made of, who has seen
          it since, and where a surface-current model puts it today.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Registry's working principle is that a file is not closed because it has gone quiet.
          Three hundred and thirty-two of the four hundred bottles in the founding series were
          never heard from again. They are still on the books, and they will stay there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------- history */}
  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="label">01 — Origin</p>
      <h2>It started as a school exercise book</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Between 1971 and 1974 a longshoreman working the Columbia River bar kept a record of every
        bottle that came ashore on one stretch of the Long Beach Peninsula: the date, the state of
        the glass, and whatever was legible on the label. By the time the book was full it held
        eleven hundred entries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read in order, those entries describe the northern arm of the North Pacific Subtropical
        Gyre — the seasonal rhythm of it, the lag after a hard winter, the way arrivals from one
        direction stop for a year and then resume. Nobody set out to measure a current. Somebody
        set out to be tidy about a beach.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Office was founded in the spring of 1974 to keep the book going, and its first act was
        <a href="/registry/DR-1974-0001">Series A</a>: four hundred numbered bottles put over the
        side outside the bar, each with a reply card. Sixty-eight cards came back. The last of them
        arrived from the Marshall Islands seven years later.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* -------------------------------------------------------------- method */}
  <section class="wrap section" id="method" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="head">
      <div class="head__meta">
        <p class="label">02 — Method</p>
        <p class="label">Last full re-solve {fmtDate(SOLVED_ON)}</p>
      </div>
      <h2>How a position is arrived at</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every present-day position on this site is computed, not written down. A single module
        holds the model; it runs once at build time to solve the whole registry, and again in your
        browser when you use the <a href="/drift">simulator</a>. The two agree because they are the
        same code.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="method">
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A kinematic surface field</h3>
          <p>
            {GYRES.length} closed rotations, {JETS.length} boundary currents including the Gulf
            Stream, the Kuroshio and the Agulhas, four zonal bands, Ekman convergence toward each
            gyre core, and a westward-propagating eddy field. The whole thing is analytic — there
            is no reanalysis dataset behind it and none is claimed. Mean speed over the ocean
            surface comes out at about 0.15 m/s, and the Gulf Stream core at about 1.7 m/s.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Windage, which is the whole point</h3>
          <p>
            An object takes on a share of the wind's push in proportion to how much of it stands
            above the waterline. The Registry models {DRIFTERS.length} profiles, from a drift
            bottle at {DRIFTERS[0].windage.toFixed(2)} to a drift card at{' '}
            {DRIFTERS[DRIFTERS.length - 1].windage.toFixed(2)}. This is not a refinement. Two
            objects released in the same wave with different windage finish on different
            continents, and the {' '}
            <a href="/registry/DR-1994-0022">shoes and the hockey gloves</a> are the proof.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Real coastlines</h3>
          <p>
            Land is Natural Earth at 1:50 million, rasterised at build time into a{' '}
            {LAND_MASK_W}×{LAND_MASK_H} bitmask — half a degree, about 55 km. Objects that hit it
            try to slide along the coast; failing that, they either strand or are lifted off on the
            next tide. A single identified object strands readily. A population of twenty-eight
            thousand keeps a reservoir offshore and goes on arriving for decades, which is what the
            record actually shows.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Ensembles, not trajectories</h3>
          <p>
            Every file is solved {ENSEMBLE_MEMBERS} times from a release box a few tens of
            kilometres across — which is about how precisely any of these positions is really
            known. The chart draws all {ENSEMBLE_MEMBERS}. The heavy line is the member most
            consistent with the sighting log; the faint ones are the other solutions, and where
            they fan out across an ocean, that fan is the honest answer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The model is scored, in public</h3>
          <p>
            Each reported find is compared against the ensemble and the miss distance is printed on
            the file. Across the whole registry the median residual is about{' '}
            <strong>{medianResidual.toLocaleString('en')} km</strong>, on drifts running to tens of
            thousands. Some files are wrong by a great deal more, and those numbers are shown too.
            A model whose errors are hidden is a decoration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="method__n num">06</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Where the model and the file disagree</h3>
          <p>
            {dissenting.length} of {CASES.length} files carry a status the ensemble does not
            support. Those pages say so, at the top, in a box. The filed status stands, because the
            archive is a record of what people concluded and a model is not a person.
          </p>
          <p class="method__list">
            {
              dissenting.map((c, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>}
                  <a href={`/registry/${c.code}`}>{c.code}</a>
                </>
              ))
            }
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- vocabulary */}
  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="label">03 — Controlled vocabulary</p>
      <h2>Six statuses and five classes</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="vocab">
      <div>
        <p class="label" style="margin-bottom:var(--sp-3)">Status</p>
        <dl class="dl">
          {
            STATUSES.map((s) => (
              <div>
                <dt>
                  <span class={`pill pill--${s.token}`}>{s.label}</span>
                </dt>
                <dd>{s.gloss}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="label" style="margin-bottom:var(--sp-3)">Class</p>
        <dl class="dl">
          {
            CLASSES.map((c) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{c.id} — {c.label}</dt>
                <dd>{c.gloss}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- disclosure */}
  <section class="wrap section" id="disclosure" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="label">04 — Disclosure</p>
      <h2>What is real here, and what is not</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="warn">
      <p>
        <strong>The Office of Pelagic Recovery does not exist.</strong> It has no premises, no
        staff, no legal standing and no relationship with any real body. Ida Vervoort is invented.
        The founding exercise book is invented. Every field note, sighting log entry and internal
        memo on this site was written for it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--sp-5)">
      <p>Each case file carries one of three marks, and the mark is load-bearing:</p>
    </div>

    <dl class="dl basisdl">
      <div>
        <dt><span class="basis basis--documented">documented</span></dt>
        <dd>
          Built around a real, publicly recorded loss event — the 1990 shoe spill, the 1992 bath
          toys, the Tokio Express containers, the Tōhoku debris field, the Misawa dock, the MSC
          Napoli, the X-Press Pearl. The event is real. The Registry's involvement in it is not.
          <span class="basisdl__n num">{basisCounts[0].n} files</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span class="basis basis--reconstructed">reconstructed</span></dt>
        <dd>
          Assembled from the general shape of things that demonstrably happen — deliberate drift
          releases, the long tail of Japanese glass floats — but not traceable to one recorded
          incident. Positions are inferred.
          <span class="basisdl__n num">{basisCounts[1].n} files</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span class="basis basis--speculative">speculative</span></dt>
        <dd>
          Invented outright, including the toy boat, the letter drop and the cabin door. They obey
          the same physics as everything else, which is the only sense in which they are true.
          <span class="basisdl__n num">{basisCounts[2].n} files</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--sp-5)">
      <p>
        The oceanography is not fiction. Surface gyres, western boundary intensification, Ekman
        convergence and windage are real and well described, and beachcombing genuinely was used to
        calibrate drift models — the shoe and bath-toy spills are the standard examples. What is
        invented is an institution to care about them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ colophon */}
  <section class="wrap section" id="colophon" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="label">05 — Colophon</p>
      <h2>How it was made</h2>
    </div>

    <dl class="dl colophon">
      <div>
        <dt>Build</dt>
        <dd>Astro, static output. No UI framework and no client router.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Interactivity</dt>
        <dd>Hand-written TypeScript modules, loaded per page. Canvas 2D for the drift field.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>src/lib/ocean.ts</code> — one module, run in Node at build time and in the browser
          at runtime.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Geography</dt>
        <dd>
          Natural Earth land polygons via <code>world-atlas</code>, public domain. Simplified to SVG
          paths and rasterised to a land mask at build time; no runtime fetch.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Projection</dt>
        <dd>Plate carrée throughout, so every chart shares one coordinate system.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Typefaces</dt>
        <dd>
          Newsreader for display, Public Sans for interface, IBM Plex Mono for soundings and
          registry codes. Self-hosted.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Colour</dt>
        <dd>
          Two themes. <em>Chart</em> is paper and ink; <em>Night watch</em> is the same chart under
          a lamp. Both are first-class and the toggle is in the masthead.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Motion</dt>
        <dd>
          Slow by policy. Everything honours <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, including the
          drift field, which drops its tracer count and stops animating in.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Images</dt>
        <dd>None. Every mark on this site is drawn from data or from type.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--sp-6)">
      Further reading, all real: Curtis Ebbesmeyer and Eric Scigliano, <em>Flotsametrics and the
      Floating World</em>; the OSCURS surface-current model; Tracey Williams's long record of the
      Tokio Express Lego on Cornish beaches; and the published literature on the North Pacific
      Subtropical Convergence Zone.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .a__h {
    font-size: var(--step-5);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-5);
  }

  .method {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: m;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .method li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr;
    gap: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4);
    padding: var(--sp-5) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
  }

  .method__n {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-3);
    padding-top: 0.25rem;
  }

  .method h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-2);
  }

  .method p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .method p + p {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  }

  .method__list {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2) !important;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .method li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--sp-2);
    }
  }

  .vocab {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-6);
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .vocab {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--sp-7);
    }
  }

  .vocab .dl > div {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 38%) 1fr;
  }

  .warn {
    padding: var(--sp-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--signal);
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--signal) 6%, transparent);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .warn p {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .basisdl {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .basisdl > div {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 26%) 1fr;
  }

  .basisdl dd {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .basisdl__n {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .colophon {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .colophon code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 0.1em 0.3em;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
</style>
